<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Required once the app is signed with a real identity under the hardened
         runtime. Ad-hoc signing is not held to this, which is why `app_script` kept
         working without it and would have stopped the moment the identity changed:
         every outgoing Apple Event needs this entitlement, and tier 1 is the whole
         differentiator. NSAppleEventsUsageDescription in Info.plist is the other
         half — the entitlement permits the event, the string explains the prompt. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The same trap as the one above, in the one place it is hardest to see.
         Under the hardened runtime macOS does not *prompt* for a resource the code
         is not entitled to reach — it denies outright, immediately, with no dialog.
         So `AVCaptureDevice.requestAccess(for: .audio)` returned false without ever
         showing anything, the grant went straight from "not requested yet" to
         "denied", and the settings panel reported that accurately: the answer really
         was no, and no amount of clicking Request could change it.

         It went unnoticed because the microphone had never worked anyway — the
         nine-channel downmix meant a granted session heard silence too, so the two
         defects presented identically. NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist is
         the other half, as with Apple Events: the entitlement permits the access, the
         string explains the prompt.

         A denial already recorded by TCC survives adding this. Clear it once with
         `tccutil reset Microphone com.openclicky.app`. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
